This is an interesting issue, and seems to be related to the tooltip proxy and the OverlayPortal's deferred layout. This is probably due to Tooltip adopting OverlayPortal in the most recent update. I'm not sure if it is common to show a tooltip while dragging, but I also am not sure if there's a way to disable the tooltip while dragging.

@kwhite-sdi if this is blocking anything, here's a quick workaround that seems to work:

```dart
 @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return ReorderableListView.builder(
      buildDefaultDragHandles: false,
      itemCount: items.length,
      onReorderEnd: (index) {
        Tooltip.dismissAllToolTips();
      },
      itemBuilder: (context, index) {
        final config = items[index];
        return ReorderableDragStartListener(
          key: ValueKey(items[index]),
          index: index,
          child: Tooltip(
            message: 'test $index',
            child: ListTile(title: Text(config)),
          ),
        );
      },
      onReorderItem: (int oldIndex, int newIndex) {
        setState(() {
          final item = items.removeAt(oldIndex);
          items.insert(newIndex, item);
        });
      },
    );
  }
```

This appears to work even with long exit durations. Alternatively, you could insert an Overlay in the proxy decoration to separate the MenuAnchor overlay from the Tooltip overlay.

@kwhite-sdi Sorry that didn't work! Here's the overlay solution I was thinking of. Let me know if that works. If not, I'll see if I can think up something else. Also, everything within the animated builder is just a copy of the default proxy builder -- the only part that actually fixes the bug is the Overlay and OverlayEntry. In other words, feel free to modify the appearance of everything within the animated builder. It shouldn't affect the fix.

Also, I _think_ this is a non-issue, but the tooltip shown while dragging is being painted outside the bounds of its child. Since we set the clip behavior to none, it still is painted, so I don't think this has any practical effect on users. It's not an "ideal" solution, but it should work and appears to address the fundamental issue causing the error.


```dart
@override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return ReorderableListView.builder(
      buildDefaultDragHandles: false,
      itemCount: items.length,
      proxyDecorator: (child, index, animation) {
        return Overlay(
          alwaysSizeToContent: true,
          clipBehavior: .none,
          initialEntries: [
            OverlayEntry(
              canSizeOverlay: true,
              builder: (context) {
                return AnimatedBuilder(
                  animation: animation,
                  builder: (BuildContext context, Widget? child) {
                    final double animValue = Curves.easeInOut.transform(animation.value);
                    final double elevation = ui.lerpDouble(0, 6, animValue)!;
                    return Material(elevation: elevation, child: child);
                  },
                  child: child,
                );
              },
            ),
          ],
        );
      },
      itemBuilder: (context, index) {
        final config = items[index];
        return ReorderableDragStartListener(
          key: ValueKey(items[index]),
          index: index,
          child: Tooltip(
            message: 'test $index',
            child: ListTile(title: Text(config)),
          ),
        );
      },
      onReorderItem: (int oldIndex, int newIndex) {
        setState(() {
          final item = items.removeAt(oldIndex);
          items.insert(newIndex, item);
        });
      },
    );
  }
```

issue.body:
### Steps to reproduce

1. Run the `main.dart` below (it calls `SemanticsBinding.instance.ensureSemantics()`, as an active screen reader would), or run the `flutter test` snippet.
2. Reorder the list (press "Reverse" / second `pumpWidget`).

Each `ListView` item is an un-keyed `Column` (header + row). The row inside carries a `GlobalKey` and contains a `Tooltip`; it moves from item index 2 to item index 1.

### Expected results

The list reorders without an exception.

### Actual results

The assertion in `SemanticsOwner.sendSemanticsUpdate` fires (debug/test only, it is an `assert`):

```
'package:flutter/src/semantics/semantics.dart': Failed assertion: line 5016 pos 13:
'!_traversalParentNodes.containsKey(node._traversalParentIdentifier) ||
 _traversalParentNodes[node.traversalParentIdentifier!] == node':
The traversalParentIdentifier must be unique. No two semantics nodes can share the same traversalParentIdentifier.
```

### Analysis

- Every `ListView` item is wrapped in `IndexedSemantics`; its semantics node is bound to the *index* and absorbs everything mergeable inside the item, including the `OverlayPortal`'s `Semantics(traversalParentIdentifier: this)` of the `Tooltip` (`SemanticsConfiguration.absorb`).
- The keyed row moves between items on reorder, so the identifier moves from one live node (old index) to another live node (new index).
- `sendSemanticsUpdate` sorts the dirty nodes by depth only, and while visiting a node it only removes the `_traversalParentNodes` entries that point to *that node itself*. If the new holder is visited before the old holder, the entry still maps to the old node and the assertion fires, although the state after the flush would be consistent.
- It is order dependent: moving the Tooltip row from index 2 to 1 fails, from index 1 to 2 passes.
- It does not happen when the row is the item itself (no un-keyed wrapper), with `addSemanticIndexes: false`, or when the `Tooltip` is wrapped in `Semantics(container: true)` (this is our workaround).
- Possible fix (untested): in the dirty-node loop, first remove the stale `_traversalParentNodes` entries of *all* dirty nodes, then register the identifiers in a second pass.

Tested only on 3.47.0 stable; I have not tried beta/master.

Related: #191173 describes the same assertion on 3.47.0 and was closed by the stale bot for lack of a reproduction (this issue is that reproduction). #190344 is a different case (two Tooltips merged into one item node, `absorb` keeps only the first identifier).

### Code sample

<details open><summary>main.dart</summary>

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';
import 'package:flutter/semantics.dart';

void main() {
  // Semantics must be enabled (as with VoiceOver/TalkBack/Narrator).
  SemanticsBinding.instance.ensureSemantics();
  runApp(const MyApp());
}

class MyApp extends StatefulWidget {
  const MyApp({super.key});
  @override
  State<MyApp> createState() => _MyAppState();
}

class _MyAppState extends State<MyApp> {
  List<String> order = ['a', 'b', 'p', 'd'];
  final keys = <String, GlobalKey>{};

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return MaterialApp(
      home: Scaffold(
        appBar: AppBar(
          actions: [
            TextButton(
              onPressed: () => setState(() => order = order.reversed.toList()),
              child: const Text('Reverse'),
            ),
          ],
        ),
        body: ListView.builder(
          itemCount: order.length,
          itemBuilder: (context, i) {
            final id = order[i];
            // Item = un-keyed Column (so its IndexedSemantics node is bound to
            // the index); the row inside is keyed with a GlobalKey and moves
            // between items on reorder.
            return Column(
              children: [
                Text('Header $id'),
                GestureDetector(
                  key: keys.putIfAbsent(id, () => GlobalKey()),
                  onTap: () {},
                  child: Row(
                    children: [
                      Text('Row $id'),
                      if (id == 'p') const Tooltip(message: 'tip', child: Text('badge')),
                    ],
                  ),
                ),
              ],
            );
          },
        ),
      ),
    );
  }
}
```

</details>

<details open><summary>flutter test</summary>

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';
import 'package:flutter_test/flutter_test.dart';

void main() {
  testWidgets('reordering a keyed row with a Tooltip trips the traversalParentIdentifier assertion', (tester) async {
    final keys = <String, GlobalKey>{};
    Widget app(List<String> order) => MaterialApp(
      home: Scaffold(
        body: ListView.builder(
          itemCount: order.length,
          itemBuilder: (context, i) {
            final id = order[i];
            return Column(
              children: [
                Text('Header $id'),
                GestureDetector(
                  key: keys.putIfAbsent(id, () => GlobalKey()),
                  onTap: () {},
                  child: Row(children: [Text('Row $id'), if (id == 'p') const Tooltip(message: 'tip', child: Text('badge'))]),
                ),
              ],
            );
          },
        ),
      ),
    );

    await tester.pumpWidget(app(['a', 'b', 'p', 'd']));
    await tester.pumpWidget(app(['d', 'p', 'b', 'a'])); // 'p' moves from index 2 to 1
    expect(tester.takeException(), isNull); // fails with the assertion below
  });
}
```

</details>

